    "result_text": "Tests green, and the audit line still carries all five fields.\n\n## What I changed\n\nI agreed with the finding \u2014 the DTO derivation was duplicated across two pipeline steps and had pushed a repository-backed `stamp()` side effect into a step whose job is logging. I applied the suggested restructure using the codebase's existing `Out<T>`/`@Val` pattern:\n\n- **New `MapOwner` step** (`MapOwner.java`) \u2014 the single place that stamps household size and maps to `OwnerDto`, publishing it via `Out<OwnerDto>`. Wired into `owners.POST.yml` after `save`, with `govern: [ transaction ]` since it does the repository `findAll`.\n- **`AuditOwnerCreated`** \u2014 now takes `@Val Owner owner` (for id/customerCode/registrationDate) and `@Val OwnerDto dto` (for the two membership fields) and only emits the log line. No more `OwnerHousehold`/`OwnerMapper` collaborators or `stamp()` call.\n- **`RespondWithOwnerCreated`** \u2014 now takes `@Val OwnerDto dto` and only responds; the derivation is gone.\n\nThe derivation lives in exactly one step, both consumers read the same published DTO (so the audit line and response body can't drift), and the second `stamp()`/`findAll()` pass is eliminated. Acceptance behaviour is unchanged: `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` \u2014 BUILD SUCCESS, with the audit line still logging `id`, `customerCode`, `registrationDate`, `membershipLevel`, and `membershipNumber`.",
